\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces In our experiment the beam of particles is a horizontal band. The beam strength is measured by a ``flux'' $\Phi = n/h$ equal to the ratio of $n$, the number of particles, to the length $h$ measured perpendicular to the beam.}}{2}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig: flux}{{3}{2}{}{figure.3}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces The geometry for a BB of impact parameter $b$ scattering off of a disk of radius $R$. The scattering angle between the initial and final direction is $\theta $.}}{3}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig: scattering mech}{{4}{3}{}{figure.4}{}}
\newlabel{eq:b(theta)}{{3}{3}{}{equation.3}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces All particles with impact parameter between $b$ and $b+\Delta b$ will be scattered with angle between $\theta $ and $\theta +\Delta \theta $. The cross sectional length $\Delta \sigma = (d\sigma /d\theta ) \Delta \theta $ is just equal to $\Delta b$.}}{3}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig: scattering}{{5}{3}{}{figure.5}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Your data tape will look something like this. The rightmost edge of the accumulation of dots to the left occurs at $\theta =0$. The angle $\theta _i$ of the i$^{th}$ bin is found by measuring the distance between the $\theta =0$ point and the center of the bin, and then dividing by the detector radius.}}{5}{}\protected@file@percent }
